// Canonical headscale ACL / Tailscale-SSH policy for the homelab mesh.
//
// NOT loaded by Nix (headscale runs `policy.mode = "database"`, see
// headscale.nix). This file is the source of truth kept under version control;
// apply it to the running coordinator out of band:
//
//   scp modules/homelab/apps/headscale-policy.hujson observability:/tmp/
//   sudo headscale policy check -f /tmp/headscale-policy.hujson   # validate
//   sudo headscale policy set   -f /tmp/headscale-policy.hujson   # store in db
//
// Re-apply after editing. See plans/headscale-mesh.md step 6.
{
  // Single-user tailnet: every node is enrolled under headscale user `phonkd`.
  // Allow full connectivity among the user's own devices...
  "acls": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["*"],
      "dst": ["*:*"],
    },
  ],
  // Exit nodes advertised by our own nodes are approved automatically, so the
  // approval is version-controlled here rather than surviving only as
  // out-of-band `headscale nodes approve-routes` state in the coordinator DB.
  //
  // This does NOT route anything: an approved exit node is merely *offered*.
  // Traffic only moves when a client explicitly runs
  // `tailscale set --exit-node=...`, which nothing in nix ever does. Today only
  // 201-mono advertises (modules/tailnet.nix). See plans/g14-vpn.md.
  //
  // NB "routes" is deliberately absent: auto-approving subnet routes is the
  // thing that could hijack the Mac's work traffic (overlapping RFC1918) --
  // that remains the hard rule in plans/headscale-mesh.md. Exit nodes are
  // 0.0.0.0/0 and opt-in per client, which is why they are safe here.
  //
  // An exit node is a PAIR of routes, 0.0.0.0/0 + ::/0, and neither layer will
  // let you split it -- measured on headscale 0.28.0 / tailscale 1.98.10:
  //   - the client refuses to advertise one half: "0.0.0.0/0 advertised without
  //     its IPv6 counterpart, please also advertise ::/0";
  //   - `headscale nodes approve-routes -i 5 -r 0.0.0.0/0` re-approves ::/0
  //     anyway, even from a cleared state and with this autoApprover removed.
  // So "approve only the working half" is not an option, and dropping this
  // autoApprover buys nothing. It stays.
  //
  // Know the consequence: 201-mono has NO IPv6 upstream (no global v6 address,
  // no v6 default route -- the ISP gives none), so a client that selects it as
  // exit node installs a v6 default pointing at a host that cannot forward v6.
  // In the steady state this is benign: 201 has no route, the connection is
  // refused in well under a second, and Happy Eyeballs falls straight over to
  // v4. Measured from g14 over the exit node: v6 fails in 0.2-0.7 s, v4 serves
  // example.com in 0.16 s with egress correctly showing the home IP.
  //
  // It is NOT always benign. Right after the exit node is first selected,
  // tailscaled can land in a state where v6 black-holes instead of refusing --
  // `curl -6 https://example.com` hung the full 20 s timeout while v4 was fine,
  // which presents as "the exit node broke everything" because every AAAA name
  // stalls. Re-selecting the exit node (`tailscale set --exit-node=201-mono`)
  // cleared it and it did not return. An `unreachable ::/0` on 201 was tried as
  // a fix and made no difference (0.68 s with it, 0.41 s without), so it was not
  // kept -- do not re-add it thinking it helps.
  "autoApprovers": {
    "exitNode": ["phonkd@"],
  },
  // ...and Tailscale SSH into any of them, as any non-root user or root.
  // `root` is listed explicitly (autogroup:nonroot excludes it) so deploy-rs
  // activation can use tailnet SSH in Phase 2.
  "ssh": [
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src": ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst": ["autogroup:self"],
      "users": ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
    },
  ],
}
